-**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Ties:** If two or three $t_{max}$ values are equal, the ray hits an edge or corner. A strict `if/else` chain may skip cells. Solutions include stepping all tied axes simultaneously, emitting all "supercover" cells, or using a deterministic half-open convention [S1, S2, unofficial].
-*   **Zero Direction Components:** If $D_a = 0$, the ray never crosses planes on that axis [S1, unofficial]. Set $\Delta t_a$ and $T_a$ to $\infty$ to avoid branching in the loop [S1, unofficial]. Avoid $0 \times \infty$ NaNs by initializing these axes separately [S1, S2, unofficial].
-*   **Boundary Conventions:** Origins exactly on voxel planes can cause `floor` operations to place the origin in a neighboring cell [S1, unofficial]. Define a clear half-open interval convention and test points just below, on, and above boundaries [S1, S2, unofficial].
-*   **Numerical Robustness:** Large world coordinates, small direction components, and negative zero can cause precision issues [S1, unofficial]. Avoid adding random epsilons; use explicit conventions and targeted tolerance [S1, S2, unofficial].
+**Edge Cases and Pitfalls**
+*   **Zero Direction Components:** If a ray component is zero, the ray is parallel to the grid planes of that axis. The algorithm must check if the ray origin lies within the slab bounds for that axis; if not, the ray misses the grid entirely [S7, S16, unofficial]. Set `tDelta` and `tMax` to infinity for that axis [S19, S42, unofficial].
+*   **Floating Point Precision:** Naïve summation of ray positions can accumulate error. Amanatides–Woo avoids this by updating `tMax` via addition of `tDelta` rather than recomputing intersections from the origin [S22, unofficial]. However, `tMax` can still drift; some implementations use integer arithmetic or periodic re-normalization [S26, unofficial].
+*   **Boundary Conditions:** Rays starting exactly on a grid boundary may be assigned to the wrong voxel due to floating-point rounding. A small epsilon nudge or consistent flooring logic (e.g., treating intervals as $[i, i+1)$) is required [S35, S40, unofficial].
+*   **Degenerate Rays:** Rays with zero length or near-zero direction should be handled as misses or special cases to avoid division by zero [S35, unofficial].
